WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.007
(ambient music)

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.610
- I've always just tried to push myself

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.063
to see the world and explore.

00:00:09.548 --> 00:00:13.650
Eventually, I found myself
really concerned about

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.230
the fact that I was spending
my time in the outdoors

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:19.350
without doing anything
beneficial for the world.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.410
I started Adventure Scientists
believing that there would be

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.270
thousands of people like
me who love the outdoors

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:27.920
and wish there was ways they
could make a difference.

00:00:31.802 --> 00:00:33.940
(light acoustic guitar music)

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.980
We are a non-profit
organization based in Montana

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:39.090
that mobilizes thousands of people

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.670
to go out and collect scientific data

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.850
for scientists who are using it to solve

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:45.940
massive societal issues.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.510
If you can imagine thousands of people

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.430
who are out hiking and skiing
and biking and climbing

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:54.090
around the world
collecting scientific data

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.660
that has been requested by scientists

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:58.880
that will help them solve problems,

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.298
that's what we do at Adventure Scientists.

00:01:01.298 --> 00:01:03.130
(ambient music)

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:06.700
We've got about 8,000 people
a month coming to our website

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.380
asking how they can get involved

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:11.540
and we built this network by giving people

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.670
in the outdoor community a way that

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:15.933
they could make a
difference with their time.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.440
We've collected the largest data set

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.893
on earth for micro plastics.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.710
We sent out volunteers
all around the world

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:28.260
to over 100 different countries
to collect scat samples.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.870
We send these scats back
to the lab at Harvard,

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.110
and this brilliant
research team has been able

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:35.610
to narrow the search for
the genes responsible

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:39.223
for antibiotic resistance
down to less than 90 genes.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.837
(ambient music)

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.600
We've just hiked our way into
the Lolo National Forest.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.220
This is right on the edge
of Western redcedar's range,

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:00.100
and so this is a super
exciting tree for us

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.675
to core and get some genetics out of.

00:02:02.675 --> 00:02:05.760
(light acoustic music)

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.650
We're getting samples because
as the climate changes

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.530
and adapts these trees that
are right on the fringes

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.770
of the habitat are likely to have genetics

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:17.420
that could really help us
mitigate climate change

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:20.860
and the effects of climate
change on these tree species.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.440
And then the other
reason is because we want

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.810
to create methods for the
U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:30.790
to determine exactly where
any piece of wood came from

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.338
and whether it was legally
or illegally harvested.

00:02:33.338 --> 00:02:35.000
(light acoustic music)

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.370
When we are choosing projects,
the most important to us

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:41.000
is that there's gonna
be a tangible pathway

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.440
from data collection to
something being different

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:44.598
in the world.

00:02:44.598 --> 00:02:45.431
(ambient music)

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:48.644
(skis scraping against snow)

00:02:48.644 --> 00:02:51.311
(ambient music)

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:54.837
The outdoors, for me, makes me feel

00:02:54.837 --> 00:02:59.112
like there's some normalcy
in a very chaotic world.

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:02.080
(ambient music)

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.740
To take my son out here and
getting to hike with him

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:08.090
and show him this incredible
place, it's amazing.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:09.720
It just means the world to me to be able

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.990
to expose him to this
part that I love so much.

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:17.160
This is a gift and a
privilege to have this now,

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.310
and I'm not sure this
will be here forever.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.240
We need to empower scientists
with every single tool

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:28.090
available on the planet
to make them successful,

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:30.320
because our entire world and our

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.552
entire livelihood depends on it.

00:03:32.552 --> 00:03:35.219
(ambient music)

